### Why is LIKE wrong for full-text search?

`WHERE description LIKE '%home loan%'` has a leading wildcard, so no B-tree index can help and the database performs a full table scan. It also runs on the primary transactional database, competing with account updates and transfers.

```java
package com.bank.search.antipattern;

public class LikeSearchWrong {

    // WRONG: full table scan on every search query
    public static final String QUERY =
        "SELECT * FROM products WHERE description LIKE '%home loan%'";
    // Problems:
    // 1. Leading wildcard -> no index used -> full table scan
    // 2. Runs on primary DB -> competes with live transactions
    // 3. Cannot rank by relevance
    // 4. Cannot handle typos
    // 5. Gets slower as data grows
}
```

### How does an inverted index work?

The engine tokenizes each document, then builds a map from each term to a posting list of document IDs. A query looks up posting lists and intersects them.

```java
package com.bank.search.engine;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class SimpleInvertedIndex {

    // term -> set of document IDs
    private final Map<String, Set<String>> index = new HashMap<>();

    public void addDocument(String docId, String text) {
        for (String token : tokenize(text)) {
            index.computeIfAbsent(token, k -> new HashSet<>()).add(docId);
        }
    }

    public Set<String> search(String query) {
        String[] terms = tokenize(query);
        Set<String> result = null;
        for (String term : terms) {
            Set<String> postingList = index.getOrDefault(term, Set.of());
            if (result == null) {
                result = new HashSet<>(postingList);
            } else {
                result.retainAll(postingList); // intersection
            }
        }
        return result != null ? result : Set.of();
    }

    private String[] tokenize(String text) {
        return text.toLowerCase().split("\\s+");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleInvertedIndex idx = new SimpleInvertedIndex();
        idx.addDocument("P001", "Home Loan with low interest rate");
        idx.addDocument("P002", "Car Loan for salaried employees");
        idx.addDocument("P003", "Home Insurance plan");

        System.out.println("Search 'home loan': " + idx.search("home loan"));
        System.out.println("Search 'loan': " + idx.search("loan"));
    }
}
```

```text
Search 'home loan': [P001]
Search 'loan': [P001, P002]
```

### How does ranking with BM25 work?

BM25 scores each document by how frequent the query terms are in that document (term frequency) and how rare they are across all documents (inverse document frequency).

```java
package com.bank.search.ranking;

public class RelevanceExplanation {
    // Document P001: title = "Home Loan", description = "...home loan..."
    // Document P003: description = "...mentions loan once in passing..."
    // Query: "home loan"
    //
    // P001 scores higher because:
    //   - "home" appears in title (boosted field)
    //   - "loan" appears in title AND description (higher TF)
    // P003 scores lower because:
    //   - "loan" appears once in description only
    //
    // LIKE cannot do this: it returns both as equal matches.
}
```

### How does fuzzy matching handle typos?

Edit distance (Levenshtein) allows a configurable number of character edits so misspelled queries still match.

```java
package com.bank.search.fuzzy;

public class LevenshteinDistance {

    public static int distance(String a, String b) {
        int[][] dp = new int[a.length() + 1][b.length() + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= a.length(); i++) dp[i][0] = i;
        for (int j = 0; j <= b.length(); j++) dp[0][j] = j;
        for (int i = 1; i <= a.length(); i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= b.length(); j++) {
                int cost = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1) ? 0 : 1;
                dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i - 1][j] + 1,
                    Math.min(dp[i][j - 1] + 1, dp[i - 1][j - 1] + cost));
            }
        }
        return dp[a.length()][b.length()];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(distance("hoem", "home")); // 1 (swap e and m)
        System.out.println(distance("lon", "loan"));   // 1 (insert a)
        // Elasticsearch fuzziness=1 would match both
    }
}
```

```text
1
1
```

### How do you keep the index in sync with the database?

The database stays the source of truth. Sync via domain events or CDC, accepting eventual consistency.

```java
package com.bank.search.sync;

public class ProductIndexer {

    private final ElasticsearchClient esClient;

    public ProductIndexer(ElasticsearchClient esClient) {
        this.esClient = esClient;
    }

    // Event-driven: consume ProductUpdated events from Kafka
    public void onProductUpdated(ProductUpdatedEvent event) {
        esClient.index("products", event.getProductId(), event.toDocument());
    }

    // CDC: Debezium reads the DB transaction log and streams changes
    // No application code changes needed, never misses a write
}
```

### How do you handle reindexing?

Index into a new index and atomically swap an alias for zero-downtime reindexing.

```java
package com.bank.search.reindex;

public class ZeroDowntimeReindex {

    public void reindex() {
        // 1. Create new index: products-v2
        // 2. Bulk index all products from DB into products-v2
        // 3. Atomically swap alias: products -> products-v2
        // 4. Delete old index: products-v1
        // Clients always query the "products" alias, never a version.
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using LIKE on the primary database for user-facing search is the most common. The second is writing only to the index and not keeping the database as the source of truth.

```java
package com.bank.search.antipattern;

// WRONG: writing to Elasticsearch without a database backup
// If the index is corrupted, you lose data.
// The DB must be the source of truth; the index is a derived copy.
```

